@page
@using ForestChurches.Pages.Docs
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = "Admin.Read")]
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Security, keys and data – Website administrator guide";
}

<header>
    <p class="fc-eyebrow">Website administrator guide · Technical reference</p>
    <h1>Security, keys and data</h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">How Forest Churches is secured: where secrets and keys are kept, what is encrypted and how, what personal data is held, where it lives, who processes it, and the security issues that need fixing.</p>
    <div class="docs-meta"><span>Restricted page</span><span>Secret <em>names</em> only; never put secret values in documentation, tickets or chat.</span></div>
</header>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--danger">
    <span class="docs-callout__title">Act on these first</span>
    <p>The public ForestChurches repository contains live credentials, including the Azure service principal secret that unlocks Key Vault. Until that secret is rotated, assume every secret in Key Vault is known to others. See <a href="#security-findings">Security findings</a> and <a href="#rotating-secrets">Rotating secrets</a>.</p>
</div>

<h2>Overview</h2>
<div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>How it's done</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Transport</td><td>HTTPS everywhere; HTTP redirects to HTTPS; HSTS for one year with <code>includeSubDomains; preload</code>. Email app calls are HTTPS-only (enforced). SMTP uses STARTTLS when offered.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Secrets</td><td>Azure Key Vault <code>projectgreenblock</code>, read with a service principal (<code>EnvironmentCredential</code>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Passwords</td><td>ASP.NET Core Identity hashing (PBKDF2, HMAC-SHA512, 100,000 iterations, random salt). 12+ characters with mixed character types.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sessions and tokens</td><td>ASP.NET Core Data Protection, keys stored in blob storage and wrapped by a Key Vault key, rotated every 7 days.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Authorisation</td><td>Permission-based policies backed by the <code>RolePermissions</code> table; a few role-name checks for SuperAdmin.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>CSRF</td><td>Antiforgery tokens on every state-changing request (with documented exceptions).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>IDs in URLs</td><td>Protected with purpose-specific Data Protection protectors (<code>Encoder</code>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>API between apps</td><td>Short-lived (10-minute) HS256 JWTs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Admin tools</td><td>Site admin behind permissions; Hangfire behind Basic auth; ELMAH SuperAdmin only.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2>Authentication</h2>
<p>Sign-up, sign-in, two-factor authentication, cookie settings and email tokens are covered in <a asp-page="/Docs/Admin/Registration">Whitelisting, sign-up and sign-in</a>. In short: invitation-only registration, confirmed email required, 12-character passwords, optional TOTP two-factor with 10 recovery codes, Secure/HttpOnly cookies, and no account lockout.</p>

<h2 id="authorisation">Authorisation</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Every permission (<code>Admin.Read</code>, <code>UserManagement.Read</code>, …) is a named policy. <code>PermissionAuthorizationHandler</code> grants it if any of the user's current roles has that permission in <code>RolePermissions</code>.</li>
    <li>Site admin pages use <code>[Authorize(Policy = "…")]</code> on the page model; this guide's admin pages use <code>@@attribute [Authorize(Policy = "Admin.Read")]</code>.</li>
    <li>Role-name checks: Emails (<code>[Authorize(Roles = "SuperAdmin")]</code>), user edit/delete (<code>IsInRole("SuperAdmin")</code>), ELMAH.</li>
    <li>Church admin pages need a signed-in user; event pages need <code>UserEvents.*</code>. Church data is always looked up from the signed-in user, not from IDs in the request.</li>
    <li>Public APIs: <code>/api/Bulletin</code> and <code>/health</code> are anonymous; <code>/api/SystemStats</code> needs <code>Admin.Read</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Full permission list: <a asp-page="/Docs/Admin/People" asp-fragment="every-permission">Every permission</a>.</p>

<h2>Where secrets are kept</h2>
<p>Production secrets live in <strong>Azure Key Vault <code>projectgreenblock</code></strong>. Both apps connect with Azure's <code>EnvironmentCredential</code>, which reads four environment variables set on the host:</p>
<div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Environment variable</th><th>Set on</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>KEY_VAULT_URL</code></td><td>Container App, Plesk</td><td>Vault address.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AZURE_TENANT_ID</code></td><td>Container App, Plesk</td><td>Microsoft Entra tenant.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AZURE_CLIENT_ID</code></td><td>Container App, Plesk</td><td>The app registration (service principal) allowed to read the vault.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code></td><td>Container App, Plesk</td><td>That service principal's client secret: <strong>the master key to everything</strong>. Store it as a Container Apps secret, never in code or <code>launchSettings.json</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

