            <tr><td>Grafana Labs</td><td>Application logs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ChurchSuite</td><td>OAuth requests for churches that connect.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>OpenStreetMap, unpkg</td><td>Visitors' IP addresses and the map area viewed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>GitHub</td><td>Source code (public), container images.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="security-findings">Security findings</h2>
<p>Found while writing this guide (October 2026, branch <code>redesign/forest-floor</code> at <code>defd5b2</code>; EmailManagement <code>feature/delivery-status</code>). Ordered by severity.</p>
<div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Severity</th><th>Finding</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>1</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--danger">Critical</span></td><td>The <strong>public</strong> repo's <code>Properties/launchSettings.json</code> contains a value for <code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>, together with the tenant ID, client ID and Key Vault URL. With these, anyone can read Key Vault and so every production secret.</td><td>Rotate the service principal secret now; then rotate every Key Vault secret (DB, storage keys, SMTP, JWT, Google, Loki, Hangfire); remove the values from the file and from git history; consider making the repo private.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--danger">Critical</span></td><td><code>.github/workflows/deploy.yml</code> refers to a GitHub secret whose <em>name</em> is the client secret value, so the value is visible in the workflow file.</td><td>Rename to e.g. <code>AZURE_CREDENTIALS</code> after rotating (#1).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--danger">High</span></td><td>EmailManagement's JWT validation key is hard-coded, so anyone with that repo can send email as info@forestchurches.co.uk.</td><td>Load issuer, audience and key from Key Vault; rotate.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>4</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--danger">High</span></td><td><code>/Church?handler=GoogleMapsApiKey</code> returns the server's Google API key to anyone.</td><td>Remove the handler (maps use OpenStreetMap); restrict the key by API and by server IP in Google Cloud; rotate.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>5</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--danger">High</span></td><td>ChurchSuite endpoints trust a <code>user_id</code> query parameter without sign-in, and redirect to an unchecked <code>redirect_uri</code>.</td><td>Require sign-in, use the current user, allow only local return URLs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>6</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--warn">Medium</span></td><td><code>/logsHub</code> (SignalR) has no authorisation, so anyone can connect and receive live log entries (email addresses, errors).</td><td>Add <code>[Authorize(Policy = "Admin.Read")]</code> to <code>LogsHub</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--warn">Medium</span></td><td>Committed secrets for development: <code>appsettings.Development.json</code> database password (LAN server) and <code>DataProtectionKeys/*.xml</code>.</td><td>Change that password; delete the files from the repo and add them to <code>.gitignore</code>; use user-secrets.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>8</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--warn">Medium</span></td><td>No account lockout or rate limiting, so passwords can be guessed without limit.</td><td>Enable <code>lockoutOnFailure: true</code> with Identity lockout options; add ASP.NET Core rate limiting on login and contact forms.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>9</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--warn">Medium</span></td><td>Contact-form values are inserted into email HTML without encoding (HTML injection from <em>info@forestchurches.co.uk</em>).</td><td>HTML-encode all user-supplied values in <code>MailSender</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>10</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--warn">Medium</span></td><td>No CSP, <code>X-Frame-Options</code>, <code>nosniff</code> or <code>Referrer-Policy</code> headers.</td><td>Register the existing CSP middleware and add the headers.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>11</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--warn">Medium</span></td><td>The email app reads whatever template path it's given from disk and emails the result.</td><td>Accept a template <em>name</em> and map it to files in <code>./templates</code> only.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>12</td><td><span class="docs-badge docs-badge--warn">Medium</span></td><td>ChurchSuite tokens, authenticator keys and recovery codes are stored in plain text.</td><td>Encrypt with Data Protection (ChurchSuite) and enable Identity <code>ProtectPersonalData</code> or hashed recovery codes.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>13</td><td><span class="docs-badge">Low</span></td><td>Swagger is public in production on the email app.</td><td>Set <code>EnableSwaggerInProduction</code> to false.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>14</td><td><span class="docs-badge">Low</span></td><td>The Hangfire dashboard password is the same secret as the initial SuperAdmin password.</td><td>Use separate secrets, or authorise the dashboard with the site's own sign-in (<code>Admin.Read</code>).</td></tr>
            <tr><td>15</td><td><span class="docs-badge">Low</span></td><td>The external-login page logs every whitelisted email address at Information level; the Loki login ID is in code.</td><td>Remove that log line; move the ID to configuration.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>16</td><td><span class="docs-badge">Low</span></td><td>Church open/close status is changed by a GET request (no antiforgery).</td><td>Change to POST.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>17</td><td><span class="docs-badge">Low</span></td><td>SMTP uses <code>StartTlsWhenAvailable</code> (could fall back to plain text).</td><td>Use <code>SecureSocketOptions.StartTls</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="rotating-secrets">Rotating secrets</h2>
<p>Most secrets are read once at start-up, so after changing one in Key Vault, <strong>restart the app</strong> (create a new Container Apps revision, or restart the Plesk app).</p>
<div class="docs-table-wrap">
    <table class="docs-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Secret</th><th>How to rotate</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Service principal (<code>AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>)</td><td>Entra ID → App registrations → the app → Certificates &amp; secrets → New client secret. Update the Container App secret/env var, the Plesk env var and the GitHub Actions secret used by <code>azure/login</code>. Restart both apps. Delete the old secret.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>db-connection-live</code></td><td>Change the database user's password, update the secret, restart.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Storage keys (<code>blob-connection-string</code>, <code>storage-account-key</code>)</td><td>Rotate the <em>secondary</em> key, switch the secrets to it, restart, then rotate the primary. Existing image links (30-minute SAS) stop working, which is harmless.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>data-key</code></td><td>Create a new key version in Key Vault and update the identifier. <strong>Keep old versions enabled</strong>; they're needed to unwrap older keys in the ring.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>JWT (<code>jwt-token-*</code>)</td><td>Generate a long random key (32+ bytes). Today: update Key Vault <em>and</em> the email app's <code>Program.cs</code>, deploy the email app and restart both together. After fixing #3, Key Vault only.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>smtp-auth-password</code></td><td>Change the mailbox password at IONOS and update the secret. Read on every send, so no restart.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>sys-admin-password</code></td><td>Update the secret and restart: changes the Hangfire login. It does <strong>not</strong> change the SuperAdmin account's password (change that in Church admin → Change Password).</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>google-api-key</code></td><td>Create a new restricted key in Google Cloud, update the secret, delete the old key.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>lokikey</code></td><td>Create a new Grafana Cloud access policy token with logs:write, update the secret, restart, revoke the old token.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2>If you suspect a breach</h2>
<ol class="docs-steps">
    <li>Rotate the service principal secret first, then the secret you think is affected (or all of them).</li>
    <li>Check Key Vault's activity log and the storage account's logs for unexpected access.</li>
    <li>In Site admin → Users, look for unexpected accounts or roles; on Roles, check no permissions were added.</li>
    <li>Search Logs and Grafana for unusual sign-ins, admin actions and errors.</li>
    <li>To sign everyone out, rotate the Data Protection key ring (this also breaks outstanding email links).</li>
    <li>If personal data may have been exposed, the data controller must consider reporting to the ICO within 72 hours.</li>
</ol>
